  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 223 AM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nHeading into the second half of the weekend and into early next week \nthe latest global and ensemble guidance suite remains in pretty \ngood agreement with shifting the aforementioned mid level trough \noffshore, however, the mid level trough axis will remain near by \njust to the northeast of the region for Sunday into Monday. At the \nsurface, the weakening cold front will eventually stall out over the \nDeep South and Northern Florida. This will allow for the synoptic \nwind flow to become more south southwesterly during this time frame. \nDeep tropical moisture advection will continue through Sunday and \nMonday as well and the mid level steering flow will remain light. \nWith the best mid to upper level support remaining well off to the \nnortheast of the region, convective initiation will remain sea \nbreeze driven each day during this time frame, with the highest \nchances of showers and thunderstorms shifting over the interior and \neast coast metro areas during the afternoon and evening hours. \nChances for strong thunderstorm development will remain rather \nlimited, however, heavy downpours with high rainfall rates combined \nwith slow storm motion will keep the potential for localized \nflooding in place especially across the urban and low lying areas. \nHigh temperatures for the second half of the weekend into early next \nweek will generally rise into the lower to mid 90s. \n\nMoving into the middle portion of the week, mid level troughing \ngradually begins to lift northeastward further away from the region \nand mid level ridging will slowly try to work back into the area. At \nthe surface, the stalled frontal boundary to the north will \ngradually wash out as high pressure builds back into the region. \nThis will allow for a return to a light easterly synoptic wind flow \nduring this time frame across most areas. Leftover moisture from \nwhat is left of the old frontal boundary to the north will continue \nto pool over South Florida, especially on Tuesday. The latest \nguidance suite is hinting at moisture levels dropping a bit on \nWednesday as the building mid level ridge increases subsidence \nacross the region. With mid level heights slowly rising, and mid \nlevel temperatures slowly warming, strong thunderstorm development \nwill remain very limited during this time frame and the main hazards \nwith thunderstorms will be the potential for heavy downpours and \nfrequent lightning each afternoon and evening. High temperatures on \nTuesday and Wednesday will rise into the lower 90s across the east \ncoast metro areas, and into the mid 90s across interior portions of \nSouthwest Florida.",
